# Small exploration wave, 14 September 2026

Three small experiments completed and passed focused independent review. Equalizing polygon support heights produced a small, heterogeneous paired
gain. A fixed rotation policy improved several starting bodies but lost to drawing
fresh shapes under the tested allocation. The first thin CEM-versus-IID run
stopped on unsupported input geometry. A separately frozen compatibility-corrected
run completed and CEM beat IID on both reported quality measures in all three seeds.

These are additions to the [reviewed existing evidence](datascience-review-2026-09-14.md),
not a replacement for that evidence or a determination that exploration should stop.
The question remains whether AI-assisted exploration helps choose or change bodies
in ways that advance the mathematical search. Methods supply evidence about that
attempt; their count is not its measure of success.

## Completed results

| Experiment | Complete design | Finding | Measured machine cost |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| [Paired support-height changes](../methods/paired-tangentialization/README.md) | Eight latent pairs each in 4×4 and 4×6; baseline/q-only/p-only/both, 64 successful evaluations | Both-minus-baseline mean +0.0152685; 11/16 positive. Bucket means +0.0039814 and +0.0265556. Negative effects remain. | Cached build 2.14s; complete construction 1.91s; evaluator 1.41s; evaluator peak RSS about 35MiB. |
| [Rotation versus fresh shapes](../methods/orientation-allocation/README.md) | Two blocks each in 3×3 and 4×4; 16 requests per arm, shared starts; 124 unique evaluations / 128 charged memberships | IID wins all four final-max comparisons. Orientation-minus-IID mean −0.144928; rotation improves three starting bodies but costs more in every block. | Cached build 3.19s; generation .57s; evaluator 13.29s; evaluator peak RSS about 35MiB. |

The first experiment tests a geometric intervention, not competing best-of-budget
policies. It conditions on all four arm geometries being admissible before target
evaluation. It does not establish universal benefit from tangentialization. Its
maximum recorded ratio is .741470.

The second tests one fixed orientation allocation on four fresh bases. It includes
the cost of a U(2) control in each orientation arm. It does not test an optimized
or adaptive orientation policy, or a population distribution over all shapes.
All geometric and U(2) controls passed. Its maximum recorded ratio is .773536.
Neither experiment found a value above one.

The [supported-v2 CEM comparison](../methods/diagonal-cem-pilot/supported-v2-report.md)
used 256 charged calls per arm in each of three seeds. Each seed shared its first
64 IID candidates between arms; later CEM populations used the existing diagonal
update, with population 64, 16 elites, four generations, smoothing .5 and variance
floor .05. All 1,344 actual requests succeeded, representing 1,536 charged calls.

| Seed suffix | IID maximum | CEM maximum | IID best-eight median | CEM best-eight median | IID / CEM measured arm seconds |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 301 | .759574 | .798513 | .728815 | .782274 | 50.62 / 35.29 |
| 302 | .810904 | .831348 | .753788 | .776334 | 47.41 / 35.45 |
| 303 | .760068 | .855655 | .722310 | .813742 | 46.21 / 36.45 |

Arm seconds include generation, updates and evaluator processes, with shared
initialization charged to both arms. CEM achieved better terminal values in less
measured process time in each pair. This is not an equal-wall-budget design or a
general optimizer ranking. The corrected run was exposed to v1 and is explicitly
not an untouched confirmatory replication.

The complete v2 run took 215.797 seconds: 182.245 seconds generating bodies,
.053 seconds updating CEM, and 32.915 seconds in evaluator processes. The latter
includes 26.805 seconds of individual request clocks. All 4,407 construction
attempts and 3,063 rejections are preserved. Two proposals failed the new
target-free evaluator-admission check. No target failed or exceeded one.

Independent review reconciled all 21 batches, nine updates (including circular
phase), frozen identities, population/elite links, costs and final metrics. The
original failed run and its matching source/binary bytes remain separately
preserved. This supplies an actual small adaptive-search result where the older
prototype supplied only implementation and failed-execution evidence.

Both use the [current arbitrary-body evaluator](../methods/current-body-evaluator/README.md):
exact stored binary64 geometry, capacity bounds from the current production API,
and numerical volume from that same reconstructed geometry. The resulting ratio
remains numerical. Historical tables are not retroactively upgraded by these runs.

## What was checked

The independent reviewer checked all paired factor/normal relationships,
normalization, input/result joins, complete memberships, transformed coordinates,
controls, source/input identities, scalar formulas, cost accounting and reported
contrasts. No material experiment-logic defect was found. There were no failed or
dropped evaluations. The reviewer did not rerun capacity computations or audit the
capacity library's mathematical correctness.

Orientation dispatch followed actual geometry. Some non-symplectic rotations
preserve coordinate-product form; the actual route mix was 72 product and 52
general requests. Reporting all rotated bodies as general would be incorrect.
The timing comparison uses full request and proposal costs, including shared-start
cost charged to both arms, rather than only the capacity subroutine's time.

## Execution and next use

Implementations were developed in parallel. Evaluator batches ran serially under
a shared lock with two-thread limits, preserving interpretable request timings.
Inputs and designs were fixed before targets. Each packet retains source,
commands, construction failures, geometry, results, validation and actual costs.

Jörn corrected the coordinator's initial use of long stop ceilings in place of
resource estimates, and its unnecessary approval/serial-planning step for these
small tasks. The observed work fit a small edit/run/write-up operation. Machine
costs above are measured; model-token costs and exact authoring/review elapsed
cost were not instrumented. Future small tasks should receive brief substantive
assessment, use parallelism where helpful, and escalate concrete constraints or
scope expansion rather than acquire a default approval gate.

The first wave now supplies three distinct observations: a heterogeneous paired
shape effect, an unfavorable fixed rotation allocation, and a favorable small
adaptive product-search comparison. None resolves the global threshold search
or establishes that further exploration should stop. The fresh two-page prose
sample is built and remains fixed while Jörn reviews its reading experience.
The next mathematical writing input being recovered is the missing concrete HKO
geometry, volume derivative and a worked feasible-section certificate row.
